this space. They do not imply different products or channels, so the score is not 3."}, "assumptions_predictions": {"status": "scored", "score": 2, "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"], "reason": "The predictions are observable: purchase intent at least 20% higher than for a standard scale, and the reason respondents cite. The 20% threshold is an assumption, which is allowed. 'Carbohydrate measurement features' is undefined, and the assumption that 'Diabetes awareness ... growing' has no source. Failing the intent prediction would trouble T1, but T1's core value-capture assumption about the direct sales force is not stated as testable."}, "evidence_judgement": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A14", "A16"], "reason": "A11 and A14 relabel verified_observation sources as 'independent_study', so the separation of source types is inaccurate. A11 correctly says the NHS excerpt does not show willingness to pay. However, that excerpt endorses ordinary 'digital kitchen scales', not a differentiated premium product, and A16 still rates T1 'likely' on that single source. A12 calls mettler-ar25-channels 'neutral'. In fact it undercuts both theories' direct-sales-force value capture. Relevant sources are never assessed: mettler-techgearlab-small-loads (a possible precision gap), mettler-dcn-acaia (an existing funded competitor) and mettler-ar25-ohaus (an existing low-cost brand). No assessment addresses the date, place or customer-group fit of the NHS (UK) source."}, "seeking_challenge": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["A13"], "reason": "A13 runs a search against T1 and returns mettler-atk-tolerance and others. Nothing found is reported or assessed afterwards, and there is no statement that nothing contrary was found. The search is not followed through, so it is not a genuine report of challenge evidence."}, "experiment_design": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["A15"], "reason": "A15 compares willingness to pay under carbohydrate-accuracy messaging and under convenience messaging. That can distinguish which message works better, but it cannot answer the actual decision. Both theories could be wrong about any demand for a Mettler premium consumer scale, and the test has no baseline against existing scales and no channel test. The decision rule jumps from a relative messaging gap to 'proceed to develop' the proposition. It mixes '10%' and '10% points'. It pools a mixed panel even though T1 predicts an effect specific to people with diabetes. The 18,000 euro cost and sample size of 300 are assumptions. Stated willingness to pay is not purchase behaviour."}, "revision": {"status": "not_applicable", "score": null, "event_ids": [], "reason": "Revision is excluded from packet.applicable_dimensions, and no update was scheduled."}, "recommendation": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["A16", "A17"], "reason": "A17 takes a position and names a next action and reversal conditions. It recommends 'Invest' while admitting that market size and willingness to pay are unknown, and the investment is not made contingent on the test. It keeps the direct sales-force route that conflicts with mettler-ar25-channels. One reversal condition, finding a viable e-commerce channel, would not logically reverse the recommendation. The position does not follow from the evidence."}}, "red_flags": [{"kind": "unsupported_inference", "event_ids": ["A9", "A16", "A17"], "reason": "T1 is rated 'likely' and investment is recommended on the strength of one NHS excerpt about ordinary digital scales. The excerpt does not establish demand for a differentiated or premium product."}, {"kind": "unsupported_inference", "event_ids": ["A9", "A10", "A17"], "reason": "Value capture through the 'existing sales force' contradicts mettler-ar25-channels, which says less complicated products are sold through indirect channels. 'Diabetes awareness ... growing' and 'carbohydrate measurement features' have no source."}, {"kind": "other", "e